1
00:00:00,462 --> 00:00:02,940
Imagine arriving at an empty workshop.

2
00:00:03,055 --> 00:00:10,465
You know how to cut, assemble, and repair things, but none of those skills works well without tools and materials.

3
00:00:10,581 --> 00:00:14,541
One behavior gathers resources and prepares the workspace.

4
00:00:14,657 --> 00:00:18,892
Other behaviors use those resources to produce different outcomes.

5
00:00:19,007 --> 00:00:23,211
Both are useful, but they optimize different immediate objectives.

6
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:30,291
Skills Estimate Empowerment, abbreviated SEE, turns this distinction into a two-policy recipe.

7
00:00:30,407 --> 00:00:35,836
A skill policy probes the distinguishable futures available from a starting context.

8
00:00:35,952 --> 00:00:42,012
A separate potential-empowerment policy learns to visit states where the skill-based estimate is high.

9
00:00:42,127 --> 00:00:48,084
The same state may therefore be an endpoint for preparation and a starting point for skill execution.

10
00:00:48,373 --> 00:00:52,769
Our workshop is a teaching example, not a reported environment.

11
00:00:52,885 --> 00:01:01,736
It highlights the key architectural separation: the skill latent describes what to do, while the outer policy improves where skills can

12
00:01:01,851 --> 00:01:02,366
begin.

13
00:01:02,481 --> 00:01:08,589
Simply switching among diverse skills does not automatically implement that outer preparation objective.

14
00:01:09,918 --> 00:01:17,469
The ideal decoder receives both the starting context and a future outcome, then predicts which skill was selected.

15
00:01:17,584 --> 00:01:22,115
A far-away outcome can mean different things depending on where the skill began.

16
00:01:22,231 --> 00:01:30,213
Conditioning on that starting context is therefore part of the mathematical channel, not an incidental implementation detail.

17
00:01:30,502 --> 00:01:36,240
As before, posterior evidence relative to the prior supplies a variational reward.

18
00:01:36,355 --> 00:01:40,403
A contrastive model can represent this evidence using logits.

19
00:01:40,518 --> 00:01:44,628
But logits are only defined relative to a normalization term.

20
00:01:44,744 --> 00:01:52,609
Subtract the log average exponential score over candidate skills to obtain a normalized density ratio for the chosen skill.

21
00:01:52,898 --> 00:01:57,175
This distinction matters when the estimate becomes another learner's reward.

22
00:01:57,291 --> 00:02:02,399
A score can grow through arbitrary offsets without representing additional control.

23
00:02:02,514 --> 00:02:09,665
The paper discusses practical classifier variants, including a joint classifier in continuous settings.

24
00:02:09,781 --> 00:02:18,711
Our derivation uses the ideal conditional form, and the lab uses exact finite distributions so every normalization can be checked

25
00:02:18,827 --> 00:02:19,531
directly.

26
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:24,684
The future state in the theory is sampled at a geometric time.

27
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:32,027
Equivalently, average the decoder reward along a rollout with discount weights and multiply by one minus gamma.

28
00:02:32,142 --> 00:02:38,457
A value function can estimate that normalized discounted return for each skill and starting context.

29
00:02:38,746 --> 00:02:40,766
Now average the skill values.

30
00:02:40,882 --> 00:02:48,632
In the ideal population calculation, this quantity lower-bounds the information realized by the current skill family.

31
00:02:48,748 --> 00:02:54,192
That realized information, in turn, cannot exceed the best available capacity.

32
00:02:54,308 --> 00:03:01,580
The first gap comes from the decoder; the second comes from skills that may not exploit every controllable outcome.

33
00:03:01,869 --> 00:03:08,720
In code, we also approximate the skill average with a finite sample and fit the value function from data.

34
00:03:08,836 --> 00:03:13,771
Those approximations are not guaranteed lower bounds on each individual estimate.

35
00:03:13,886 --> 00:03:21,436
Increasing the sample count reduces ordinary sampling variance under independence, but cannot fix a biased critic.

36
00:03:21,551 --> 00:03:27,294
The lab separates true capacity, realized information, and the estimated score.

37
00:03:28,622 --> 00:03:32,243
There is a data-collection problem hidden inside the equations.

38
00:03:32,358 --> 00:03:37,956
We cannot first solve every possible skill from every possible state in a large environment.

39
00:03:38,071 --> 00:03:44,057
We need a way to sample the contexts that matter while learning the skills that measure those contexts.

40
00:03:44,346 --> 00:03:52,211
SEE collects two-stage rollouts: run the potential policy, then sample a skill and execute it from the reached state.

41
00:03:52,327 --> 00:03:56,564
The outer trajectory proposes a promising starting context.

42
00:03:56,680 --> 00:04:00,007
The skill continuation probes its available control.

43
00:04:00,123 --> 00:04:08,941
Data from these interactions updates the representations, value estimates, and policies, producing a moving feedback loop.

44
00:04:09,230 --> 00:04:12,727
A useful debugging plot would show both phases separately.

45
00:04:12,843 --> 00:04:19,225
If preparation reaches the same state every time, ask whether its estimated advantage is real.

46
00:04:19,341 --> 00:04:27,950
If skills collapse to identical outcomes, a low empowerment estimate might reflect weak skills rather than a fundamentally powerless

47
00:04:28,066 --> 00:04:28,686
state.

48
00:04:28,802 --> 00:04:33,723
This is why recording only the outer reward curve can hide which component failed.

49
00:04:35,052 --> 00:04:40,207
Suppose an agent can wiggle an irrelevant display with extremely fine precision.

50
00:04:40,323 --> 00:04:46,557
A sufficiently expressive decoder might distinguish many skill labels from tiny display changes.

51
00:04:46,673 --> 00:04:54,277
That could create a large information score without producing the resource gathering or object manipulation that motivated the

52
00:04:54,393 --> 00:04:55,274
experiment.

53
00:04:55,563 --> 00:04:59,534
The paper therefore treats the measurement channel as a design choice.

54
00:04:59,650 --> 00:05:07,696
Its practical implementation restricts which state variables enter empowerment estimation and adds a noise-based bottleneck in

55
00:05:07,811 --> 00:05:09,166
continuous settings.

56
00:05:09,282 --> 00:05:13,589
The policy can still receive the full observation needed for control.

57
00:05:13,704 --> 00:05:19,248
What the agent can see and what counts toward the empowerment objective need not be identical.

58
00:05:19,536 --> 00:05:28,123
The data-processing inequality gives a useful intuition: adding independent measurement noise cannot increase true mutual information.

59
00:05:28,239 --> 00:05:32,286
It can suppress fragile distinctions below a chosen resolution.

60
00:05:32,402 --> 00:05:37,296
That is an inductive bias, not a proof that all remaining behavior is useful.

61
00:05:37,412 --> 00:05:44,975
In the lab, compare meaningful outcome noise with a deliberately biased empowerment estimate and observe their different effects.

62
00:05:46,303 --> 00:05:54,485
The authors report emergent behaviors in their tested environments, including resource gathering and environment modification.

63
00:05:54,600 --> 00:06:01,051
These are empirical observations about a particular method, measurement channel, and training setup.

64
00:06:01,166 --> 00:06:08,556
They are not evidence that arbitrary agents will discover every useful behavior from an unrestricted information objective.

65
00:06:08,845 --> 00:06:12,912
For your own experiment, evaluate three things independently.

66
00:06:13,028 --> 00:06:17,389
First, inspect the states reached by the preparation policy.

67
00:06:17,505 --> 00:06:23,639
Second, execute different skills from those states and measure whether their effects remain distinguishable.

68
00:06:23,755 --> 00:06:28,118
Third, evaluate any downstream task you actually care about.

69
00:06:28,233 --> 00:06:34,146
A score produced by the same learned estimator used for training is not an independent validation.

70
00:06:34,435 --> 00:06:43,316
The central algorithmic idea is reusable: let skills estimate controllable futures, then learn to seek states where that estimate is

71
00:06:43,432 --> 00:06:44,027
large.

72
00:06:44,143 --> 00:06:51,330
Its reliability depends on representation choices, data coverage, and the quality of the estimator.

73
00:06:51,446 --> 00:06:59,133
The next lesson asks what the empowerment objective itself can tell us about which state features a representation should keep.
